## PART 2: When to do each step (RAG)

The order never changes.

| Step | What | When you do it | Why this order |
|------|------|----------------|----------------|
| 1 | **Chunk** | When new documents arrive | You cannot embed one giant blob. Small focused pieces search better. |
| 2 | **Embed** | Right after chunking. Once per chunk. | Turns text into numbers so you can search by meaning. |
| 3 | **Store** | Right after embedding | Saves vectors on disk so you do not redo work after a restart. |
| 4 | **Retrieve** | Every time a question arrives | Finds the few chunks closest in meaning to the question. |
| 5 | **Generate** | After retrieving | The LLM writes a clean answer using only those chunks. |
| 6 | **Evaluate** | After steps 1 to 5 work | You cannot test a pipeline that does not exist yet. |

See Part 8 for a full card on each step (what it does, when to use it, snippet, what to edit, how to check it).

Important details:
- Steps 1 to 3 happen at **upload time**, once per document.
- Steps 4 and 5 happen at **question time**, every time.
- The question is embedded automatically by Chroma inside step 4. You do not do it yourself.
- Lower distance = closer match. If the best distance is high (for example above 0.8), the documents probably do not contain the answer.

Chunking rule of thumb (this is the part you EDIT per project):
- Clean text with blank lines between paragraphs: split on `\n\n`
- Documentation pages: split on headings
- PDFs from scanners or legal text: split on known headers, or by a fixed number of characters
- Always print the chunks and look at them before moving on.

